  [#*: [#> to=float-arithmetic: #~"+ - * /", #~"sum", #~"difference", #~"product", #~"quotient", #~"add", #~"sub", #~"mul", #~"quo", #~"sqrt", #~"neg", #~"abs"]]
  [#*: [#> to=float-precision: #~"withPrec", #~"withMode", #~"minPrec", #~"acc"]]
  [#*: [#> to=float-parts: #~"sign", #~"isInf", #~"isInt", #~"exponent", #~"mantissa", #~"scaleB n:"]]
  [#*: [#> to=float-comparison: #~"== != < <= > >=", #~"compare", #~"copy"]]
  [#*: [#> to=float-conversions: #~"i64", #~"u64", #~"f64", #~"f32"] -- exact; #~"roundF64", #~"roundF32", #~"truncInt", #~"rat", #~"ieeeBits"]
  [#*: [#> to=float-text: #~"string", #~"text format: digits:"]]


#--: Constants

#~"math.pi", #~"math.e", #~"math.phi" (the golden ratio), #~"math.sqrt2",
#~"math.sqrtE", #~"math.sqrtPi", #~"math.sqrtPhi", #~"math.ln2",
#~"math.log2E" (#~"1 / ln2"), #~"math.ln10" and #~"math.log10E"
(#~"1 / ln10") are untyped float constants, written to 70 digits, so each
lands in any float type as that type's value nearest the true constant,
rounded once: #~"math.pi" is the nearest #~"f64" where an #~"f64" is meant,
#~"math.pi.f32" the nearest #~"f32", and #~"math.pi.f128" all 113 bits of
the nearest #~"f128". Folded with other constants they stay exact until they
land: #~"math.pi / 2" is the nearest #~"f64" to half the 70-digit value.


#--: BigInt

A #~"BigInt" is a sign and a magnitude: #~"negative", and #~"words", the
magnitude's 64-bit words least significant first with no leading zero
word. Zero is the empty list and is never negative. It is a class: an
operator returns a new value, and the accumulator methods change one in
place.


#----: Construction

#~"math.BigInt from: x" takes an #~"i64". A narrower integer, signed or
not, widens to one by itself, and a literal coerces, so
#~"math.BigInt from: 5" and #~"from: k" for an #~"i8" or a #~"u32"
#~"k" read the same. A #~"u64" is #~"math.BigInt.fromU64 from: n", and
a 128-bit value is read with #~"parse" -- #~"(math.BigInt.parse text:
v.string base: 10)" -- so a program that writes no 128-bit integer
needs none, and builds under tcc, which has none.

#~"math.BigInt.parse text: base:" reads an optional sign and then digits:

#ul:
  [#*: base #b:"2" to #b:"62" reads plain digits -- #~"0-9", #~"a-z", and
    above 36 #~"A-Z" as the digits 36 to 61; up to 36 the cases mean the same]
  [#*: base #b:"0" reads a prefix -- #~"0b", #~"0o", #~"0x", or a bare
    #~"0" for octal -- and is decimal without one; only base 0 lets #~"_"
    separate digits]

The whole text must be the number. A text with no digit at all answers
#~"parseerror.empty"; anything else that is not a number in the base,
#~"parseerror.invalidDigit". Any other base panics.

#~".string" is the value in decimal and #~".text base:" in base 2 to 62,
in the digits #~"parse" reads back, a leading #~"-" when negative.


#----: Arithmetic

#~"+", #~"-" and #~"*" are exact. #~"/" truncates toward zero and #~"%"
takes the dividend's sign, as Go's #~"Quo" and #~"Rem" do:
#~"-7 / 2" is #~"-3" and #~"-7 % 2" is #~"-1". Division by zero panics.
#~"neg", #~"abs" and #~"sign" (#~"-1", #~"0" or #~"1") read as their
names say.


#----: Division

#~"div by:" and #~"mod by:" are Euclidean, as Go's #~"Div" and #~"Mod": the
remainder is never negative, so #~"-7 div 2" is #~"-4" and #~"-7 mod 2" is
#~"1".

#~"divide by: mode:" answers a [#> to=bigdivision: #~"BigDivision"], the
quotient rounded as the [#> to=roundingmode: #~"roundingmode"] asks and the
remainder with #~"quo * by + rem == this", as Go's #~"Divide":

#ul:
  [#*: #~"toZero" truncates -- the #~"/" and #~"%" pair]
  [#*: #~"toNegativeInf" floors, #~"toPositiveInf" ceils]
  [#*: #~"toNearestEven" rounds a half to the even quotient]

The two modes an integer division has no use for panic.


#----: Comparison

The comparisons and #~"compare" (#~"-1", #~"0" or #~"1") order by value.
#~"hash" agrees with #~"==", so a #~"BigInt" keys a map. #~"copy" is a
value with its own words.


#----: Conversions

#~".i64" and #~".u64" answer #~"converror.outOfRange" for a value they
cannot hold. #~".f64" answers the value when an #~"f64" holds it exactly,
#~"precisionLoss" when it falls between two, and #~"outOfRange" past the
largest.


#----: Bits

The bitwise operators read a value as two's complement of unbounded
width -- a negative value's sign bits run on forever -- as Go's
#~"And", #~"Or", #~"Xor", #~"AndNot" and #~"Not" do: #~"-1 & x" is
#~"x", and #~"x >> n" of a negative #~"x" rounds toward negative
infinity (#~"-7 >> 1" is #~"-4"). #~"bit i:" and #~"setBit i: b:" read
and set a bit of that form. #~"bitLength" and #~"trailingZeros" measure
the magnitude.


#----: Number theory

#ul:
  [#*: #~"exp y: m:" -- #~"this^y mod |m|", in #~"[0, |m|)"; a zero #~"m"
    is no modulus, and the plain power is 1 for #~"y <= 0". A negative
    #~"y" with a modulus raises the inverse to #~"|y|", and panics when
    there is none -- where Go answers nil]
  [#*: #~"gcd y:" -- never negative; #~"gcdExtended y:" answers a
    [#> to=biggcd: #~"BigGCD"] whose cofactors make
    #~"gcd == this*x + y*y'"]
  [#*: #~"modInverse n:" -- #~"(Option BigInt)": the inverse in
    #~"[0, |n|)", or none when the two share a factor]
  [#*: #~"sqrt" -- the floor of the square root; panics on a negative
    value]
  [#*: #~"jacobi y:" -- the Jacobi symbol for an odd #~"y"]
  [#*: #~"probablyPrime n:" -- #~"n" rounds of Miller-Rabin and a
    Baillie-PSW test. A prime always answers true; a composite chosen at
    random answers true with probability at most #~"4^-n", and none below
    #~"2^64" ever does. The Miller-Rabin bases come from a SplitMix64
    generator seeded by the value's low word, where Go uses #~"math/rand":
    as fixed for a given number, but not Go's. Not for a number an
    adversary chose]
  [#*: #~"BigInt.product from: to:" -- every integer from #~"from" to
    #~"to" multiplied: 1 for an empty range, 0 for one that holds 0]
  [#*: #~"BigInt.binomial n: k:" -- the binomial coefficient
    #~"C(n, k)", 0 when #~"k" is negative or past #~"n"]


#----: Accumulators

#~"acc.add x", #~"sub", #~"mul", #~"quo" and #~"rem" change #~"acc" in
place -- the forms for a loop that would otherwise build a new value
each step.

#code language=zerolang: """
  sum: function {n: u64} out math.BigInt is {
      acc: math.BigInt from: 0
      for each i: n.times loop acc.add (math.BigInt from: i * i)
      return acc
  }
  """


#--: BigDivision

A quotient and its remainder, as #~"BigInt.divide" answers them:
#~"quo * divisor + rem" is the dividend.


#--: BigGCD

A greatest common divisor and its cofactors, as #~"BigInt.gcdExtended"
answers them: #~"gcd == a*x + b*y", the #~"gcd" never negative.


#--: BigRat

A #~"BigRat" is a quotient #~"num / den" of two #~"BigInt"s, always in
lowest terms: #~"den" is positive and shares no factor with #~"num", which
carries the sign, and an integer has #~"den" 1. Equal values therefore
have equal parts, and every result is reduced before it is returned. Like
#~"BigInt" it is a class, and its operators return new values.

#code language=zerolang: """
  main: function is {
      third: math.BigRat.fromFraction num: (math.BigInt from: 1) den: (math.BigInt from: 3)
      sum: third + third + third
      print "\{sum.string} \{third.decimal digits: 5} \{third.roundF64}"
      r: math.BigRat.parse text: "0.1"
      match (r) case ok then print "\{r.string} \{r.roundF64}" case err then print "bad"
  }
  """

This prints #~"1 0.33333 0.333333" and #~"1/10 0.1".


#----: Rational construction

#~"math.BigRat from: n" takes an #~"i64", as #~"BigInt" does;
#~"fromFraction num: den:" reduces any two #~"BigInt"s, and panics on a
zero #~"den"; #~"fromInt from:" takes a #~"BigInt" whole.
#~"fromF64 from:" answers #~"(Option BigRat)": every finite #~"f64" is a
rational exactly (#~"0.1" is #~"3602879701896397/36028797018963968"), and
an infinity or a NaN is none.

#~"math.BigRat.parse text:" reads a fraction #~"\"a/b\"" -- each part
decimal or prefixed #~"0b", #~"0o" or #~"0x", the divisor unsigned and not
zero -- or a number with a radix point and an exponent: #~"\"1.5\"",
#~"\"-2.5e-3\"", #~"\"0x1.8p4\"". A decimal mantissa takes a base-10
#~"e" or a base-2 #~"p" exponent, a hex one #~"p" alone (an #~"e" is a hex
digit), and a leading #~"0" is decimal, not octal. The whole text must
be the number: #~"parseerror.empty" when it holds no digit,
#~"overflow" for an exponent past what is computed, #~"invalidDigit" for
anything else.


#----: Rational arithmetic

#~"+ - * /" are exact; #~"/" and #~"inverse" panic on a zero divisor.
#~"neg", #~"abs" and #~"sign" mean what they do on #~"BigInt", and
#~"isInt" answers whether #~"den" is 1.


#----: Rational comparison

#~"compare" and the six comparisons order by value; #~"==" compares the
parts, which lowest terms makes the same thing, and #~"hash" agrees
with it.


#----: Rational floats

#~"roundF64" and #~"roundF32" answer the nearest float, a tie to the even
one, and an infinity past the largest; the sign is the value's, a
zero's included. #~"f64" and #~"f32" answer that float only when it is the
value exactly -- #~"precisionLoss" when it lies between two, and
#~"outOfRange" past the largest -- as #~"BigInt"'s conversions do.
#~"ieeeBits mbits: ebits:" is the nearest value in any IEEE 754 binary
interchange format -- binary16 is 10 and 5, binary128 112 and 15 -- as its
bit pattern, rounded once from the exact quotient (the quotient is taken
two bits wider toward zero and made odd when inexact, which leaves the
second rounding nothing to get wrong), subnormals kept.


#----: Rational text

#~".string" is #~"\"a/b\"", or #~"\"a\"" for an integer;
#~".fraction" is #~"\"a/b\"" always. #~"decimal digits:" writes the value
with that many digits after the point, the last rounded to nearest with a
half away from zero (#~"-2/3" to 4 digits is #~"-0.6667").
#~"decimalPrecision" answers a [#> to=decimalprec: #~"decimalprec"]: how
many digits follow the point before the expansion repeats, and whether
that many are exact -- #~"1/4" has 2, exactly, #~"1/6" has 1, rounded.


#--: BigFloat

A #~"BigFloat" is a sign, a mantissa of up to #~"prec" bits and an
exponent -- #~"sign * mantissa * 2^exp" with #~"0.5 <= mantissa < 1" -- or a
signed zero, or a signed infinity. Each value carries the precision and
the [#> to=roundingmode: #~"roundingmode"] its results round to, and the
[#> to=accuracy: #~"accuracy"] of the operation that made it: whether that
result lies below, at or above the exact one. The exponent is an
#~"i32"; past it a result is an infinity, below it a zero. There are no
NaNs: an operation whose IEEE 754 result would be one -- #~"inf - inf",
#~"0 * inf", #~"0 / 0", the root of a negative -- panics, as Go's does.
At 24 or 53 bits and #~"toNearestEven" the operators give what #~"f32" and
#~"f64" arithmetic gives, for operands and results those formats hold as
normal numbers.

#code language=zerolang: """
  main: function is {
      two: math.BigFloat from: 2.0 prec: 200
      print (two.sqrt.text format: math.floatformat.fixed digits: 40)
      one: math.BigFloat.fromI64 from: 1
      three: math.BigFloat.fromI64 from: 3
      third: math.BigFloat.quotient x: one y: three prec: 24 mode: math.roundingmode.toZero
      print "\{third.string} \{third.acc.below} \{third.roundF32}"
      r: math.BigFloat.parse text: "1e-400" prec: 64
      match (r) case ok then {
          print "\{r.string} \{r.roundF64}"
          print (r.text format: math.floatformat.hex digits: -1)
      } case err then print "bad"
  }
  """

This prints #~"1.4142135623730950488016887242096980785697",
#~"0.3333333135 1 0.333333", #~"1e-400 0" and
#~"0x1.2bfcfc0f923df5f4p-1329".


#----: Float construction

Every constructor takes #~"prec:" and #~"mode:"; #~"prec: 0", the default,
picks the precision that holds the source exactly -- 53 bits for an
#~"f64", 64 for an integer, the larger of 64 and the bit length for a
#~"BigInt" -- and #~"mode" defaults to #~"toNearestEven". A smaller
precision rounds by the mode. #~"math.BigFloat from: x" takes an #~"f64",
and panics on a NaN; #~"fromI64", #~"fromU64" and #~"fromInt" take
integers; #~"fromRat" takes a #~"BigRat", its default precision the
larger of its parts' bit lengths and 64. #~"zero" and #~"inf" make a
signed zero and a signed infinity. #~"fromIEEEBits bits: mbits: ebits:"
reads an IEEE 754 interchange format of #~"mbits" stored mantissa bits and
an #~"ebits"-bit exponent -- binary16 is 10 and 5, binary128 112 and 15 --
exactly, and is none for a NaN.

#~"math.BigFloat.parse text: base: prec: mode:" reads an optional sign,
then a mantissa with an optional radix point and an optional exponent
-- #~"e" for a power of 10, #~"p" for a power of 2 -- or #~"\"Inf\"" or
#~"\"inf\"". Base 0, the default, reads a #~"0b", #~"0o" or #~"0x" prefix
and lets #~"_" separate digits; 2, 8, 10 and 16 read plain digits in that
base. A hexadecimal mantissa takes #~"p" alone, since #~"e" is one of its
digits. The value rounds once to #~"prec" bits, 0 taking 64. The whole
text must be the number: #~"parseerror.empty" when it holds no digit,
#~"overflow" for an exponent past what a #~"BigFloat" holds,
#~"invalidDigit" for anything else.


#----: Float arithmetic

The operators round to the larger operand precision by the left
operand's mode. #~"sum", #~"difference", #~"product" and #~"quotient"
take the precision and the mode their result rounds to, #~"prec: 0" again
the larger of the operands'. #~"add", #~"sub", #~"mul" and #~"quo" write
the result back into the value, at its own precision and mode: the
accumulator form for a loop. #~"sqrt" rounds the root to the value's
precision and mode, and the root of #~"-0" is #~"-0". #~"neg" and
#~"abs" change only the sign. An exact zero sum is #~"+0" but under
#~"toNegativeInf", where it is #~"-0", as IEEE 754 has it; a nonzero over
a zero is an infinity.


#----: Float precision

#~"withPrec prec:" rounds a copy to that many bits by its mode -- 0 makes a
finite value a zero of its sign -- and #~"withMode mode:" changes the mode
a copy rounds by from then on. #~"minPrec" is the fewest bits that hold
the value exactly. The #~"acc" field reads the accuracy of the operation
that made the value; a conversion's accuracy is the sign of
#~"converted.compare rhs: x".


#----: Float parts

#~"sign" is #~"-1", #~"0" or #~"1", with #~"-0" a 0; the #~"negative"
field is the sign bit, a zero's included. #~"isInf" and #~"isInt" mean
what they say. #~"exponent" and #~"mantissa" split a value into
#~"mantissa * 2^exponent" with #~"0.5 <= |mantissa| < 1"; #~"scaleB n:"
multiplies by #~"2^n" exactly unless the exponent leaves the #~"i32".


#----: Float comparison

#~"compare" and the six comparisons order by value: #~"-0" equals
#~"0", and two values of different precisions compare as numbers. There
is no #~"hash": equal values may differ in precision and mode, which is
what a #~"BigFloat" carries besides its value.


#----: Float conversions

#~".i64", #~".u64", #~".f64" and #~".f32" answer the value only when it is
the target exactly: #~"precisionLoss" for a fraction or a value between
two floats, #~"outOfRange" past the target -- an infinity included for
the integers. #~"roundF64" and #~"roundF32" answer the nearest float, a
tie to the even one, with subnormals kept; a value too small is a zero
of its sign and one too large an infinity. #~"ieeeBits mbits: ebits:" is
the same rounding into any IEEE 754 binary interchange format, as its bit
pattern. #~"truncInt" is the integer toward zero, none for an infinity,
and #~"rat" the value exactly as a #~"BigRat", none for an infinity.


#----: Float text

#~".string" is the value in general format with 10 digits.
#~"text format: digits:" spells it in a [#> to=floatformat: #~"floatformat"]:
#~"exponent" (#~"-d.dddde+dd"), #~"fixed" (#~"-dddd.dddd"), #~"general"
(the exponent form for exponents below -4 or at least the digit count,
the fixed one otherwise), their upper-case forms, #~"hex"
(#~"-0x1.dddp+dd"), #~"hexFraction" (#~"-0x.dddp+dd") and #~"binary"
(#~"-ddddp+dd", a decimal mantissa of #~"prec" bits). The digit count is
the digits after the point for #~"exponent", #~"fixed" and #~"hex", and the
digits in all for #~"general"; a negative count spells the fewest
decimal digits that read back as this value at its precision. Decimal
digits round to nearest even. An infinity is #~"\"+Inf\"" or
#~"\"-Inf\"". Go's #~"Format" and #~"Scan", the #~"fmt" package's
verbs and flags, have no counterpart here.


#--: decimalprec

The digits a rational's decimal expansion has before it repeats, and
whether that many spell it exactly, as #~"BigRat.decimalPrecision"
answers them.


#--: roundingmode

How a result between two representable values is chosen: the nearer
with a tie to the even one (#~"toNearestEven") or away from zero
(#~"toNearestAway"), toward zero, away from zero, or toward either
infinity. Every #~"math" type that rounds takes it.


#--: accuracy

How a rounded result compares to the exact value: #~"below", #~"exact"
or #~"above", as a #~"BigFloat"'s #~"acc" reports it.


#--: floatformat

How #~"BigFloat.text" spells a value, Go's format letters by name:
#~"exponent" #~"'e'", #~"exponentUpper" #~"'E'", #~"fixed" #~"'f'",
#~"general" #~"'g'", #~"generalUpper" #~"'G'", #~"hex" #~"'x'",
#~"hexFraction" #~"'p'" and #~"binary" #~"'b'".


#--: Fixed-width integers

#~"(math.int words: N)" and #~"(math.uint words: N)" hold #~"N" 64-bit
words, least significant first, in two's complement: a signed or unsigned
integer of #~"64*N" bits. They are records -- valtypes -- so they sit in
other records, in variants and in arrays as plain data, and copy as values.
#~"math.i256" and #~"math.u256" name the 4-word instances, #~"math.i512"
and #~"math.u512" the 8-word ones; any other width is written out, as
#~"(math.int words: 3)".

Their arithmetic is the #~"i64" family's. #~"+", #~"-", #~"*" and #~"neg"
wrap modulo #~"2^(64*N)"; #~"/" truncates toward zero and #~"%" takes the
dividend's sign, a zero divisor panics, and the most negative #~"int"
divided by #~"-1" wraps to itself. #~"<<" and #~">>" take a #~"u64" count:
past the width a left shift gives 0 and a right shift 0, or #~"-1" for a
negative #~"int", whose right shift copies the sign in.

#~"create from:" takes an #~"i64" for #~"int" and a #~"u64" for #~"uint",
and defaults to 0, so a bare #~"math.i256" is zero. #~"fromBig from:"
reads a #~"BigInt" and #~"parse text: base:" reads text as
#~"BigInt.parse" does; both answer a #~"resultval" -- #~"outOfRange", or
#~"parseerror.overflow" -- when the value does not fit, rather than
wrapping it. Out, #~"big" is the exact #~"BigInt", #~"string" and
#~"text base:" are its text, and #~"i64", #~"u64" and #~"f64" answer a
#~"resultval" as the #~"BigInt" conversions do.

#~"==", #~"compare" and the order operators compare the values;
#~"hash" agrees with #~"==", so either type keys a map or set over value
keys, such as #~"MapVR k: math.i256 V: String".

#code language=zerolang: """
  main: function is {
      a: math.i256 from: -1
      b: math.u256.parse text: "0xffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff" base: 0
      match b case ok then print "\{(b * b).string}" case err then print "bad"
      print "\{(a >> 255).string} \{(a.neg << 255).string}"
  }
  """

The word loops are math's kernels run over the limbs' own windows, so a
fixed-width integer costs no allocation for #~"+", #~"-", #~"*", the bit
operators and the shifts; #~"/", #~"%", the text and the conversions through
#~"BigInt" do allocate.


#--: Fixed decimals

#~"(math.decimal64 precision: P scale: S)" is SQL's #~"NUMERIC(P, S)": at
most #~"P" decimal digits, #~"S" of them after the point. Its value is its
coefficient, #~"coef", divided by #~"10^S". The four tiers differ in the
coefficient and so in how many digits they hold: #~"decimal32" an #~"i32" (9
digits, four bytes), #~"decimal64" an #~"i64" (18), #~"decimal128" a 128-bit
#~"math.int" (38) and #~"decimal256" a 256-bit one (76). Each is a record, a
valtype, and each instance is checked where it is built: a precision of 0 or
past the tier, or a scale past the precision, is a compile-time error.

#~"create from:" takes an integer, #~"fromCoef from:" the coefficient itself,
and #~"parse text:" a sign, digits and an optional point; digits past the scale
round half-even. #~"+" and #~"-" are exact. #~"*" and #~"/" take the same type
and round the result half-even back to its scale, as a bank or a database
does. Changing the precision, the scale or the tier is an explicit conversion
through the exact rational: #~"T.fromRat from: x.rat mode: m", in any
#~"roundingmode".

A result too long for the precision, or a division by zero, does not panic: it
is an #b:"invalid" decimal, sticky through every operation after it, which
prints as #~"invalid". #~"isInvalid" asks, #~"checked" answers a #~"resultval"
with the #~"decerror" -- #~"overflow" or #~"divisionByZero" -- and every
invalid value equals every other, so #~"==" and #~"hash" still hold. An
invalid value has no order, sign or numeric value: #~"compare", #~"<",
#~"sign", #~"rat", #~"f64" and the integer part panic on one.

#code language=zerolang: """
  money: (math.decimal64 precision: 12 scale: 2)

  main: function is {
      price: money.parse text: "19.99"
      match price case ok then {
          total: price * (money from: 3)
          print "\{total.string} \{(total / (money from: 0)).string}"
      } case err then print "bad price"
  }
  """

The native tiers compute in #~"i64", a product or a scaled dividend of up to
36 digits through the #~"u64" two-word multiply and divide, and allocate
nothing; #~"decimal128" and #~"decimal256" compute in #~"BigInt".


#--: Fixed binary floats

#~"(math.float words: N)" is a binary floating-point number of #~"64*N"
mantissa bits held inline: a record of its sign, its form (zero, finite or
infinite), an #~"i32" exponent and its mantissa words, so it sits in records,
variants and arrays as plain data. Its semantics are #~"BigFloat"'s at that
precision, rounding to nearest with ties to even: signed zeros, signed
infinities, no subnormals, and an operation that would give a NaN -- an
infinity minus itself, a zero times an infinity -- panics.

#~"create from:" takes an #~"f64", which every width holds exactly, and
defaults to +0; #~"fromBig", #~"fromInt" and #~"fromRat" round their value to
the width, and #~"parse text:" reads as #~"BigFloat.parse" does.
#~"==" and the order treat the two zeros as equal; like #~"BigFloat", a
#~"float" has no #~"hash". #~"big" is the exact #~"BigFloat", and #~"f64",
#~"roundF64", #~"rat", #~"truncInt" and #~"text" answer as #~"BigFloat"'s do.

#code language=zerolang: """
  main: function is {
      third: (math.float words: 2) from: 1.0
      q: third / ((math.float words: 2) from: 3.0)
      print "\{(q.text format: math.floatformat.exponent digits: 36)}"
  }
  """

Each operation reads its operands as #~"BigFloat"s of the width's precision
and rounds the result back, so it allocates as #~"BigFloat" does.


#--: Performance and toolchains

The loops every operation rests on -- add, subtract, shift and
multiply-and-add over runs of 64-bit words -- come in three tiers, picked when
the program's C is compiled; the rest of #~"math" is zerolang.

#ul:
  [#*: Assembly. On x86-64 under gcc or clang, the kernels Go's math/big
    generates, written as GNU inline asm into
    #~"src/runtime/natives/_Z_MATH_ARITH_AMD64.inc" by #~"tools/asmgen", a port
    of Go's generator. Each runs from the length where it measured faster than
    the tier below: 4 words for add and subtract, 9 for the shifts and the
    multiply by a word, 8 for multiply-and-add, which uses MULX, ADCX and ADOX
    and so runs only on a processor with ADX, asked at run time. Over long runs
    add and subtract take about 40% of the time the builtins do, shifts three
    quarters, multiply-and-add 57%. The aarch64 kernels are generated and pass
    the same tests but are compiled only with #~"-DZ_MATH_ASM_ARM64" until arm64
    hardware has measured them.]
  [#*: Compiler builtins. The C loops in #~"src/runtime/natives/_Z_MATH_ARITH.inc",
    a port of Go's portable loops, over the #~"system" word primitives, which
    take #~"unsigned __int128" for the two-word product and
    #~"__builtin_addcll" / #~"__builtin_subcll" for a carry chain where the C
    compiler has them; gcc and clang lower those to mul, adc and sbb on x86-64
    and to umulh and adcs on aarch64. #~"-DZ_MATH_NOASM" takes this tier
    everywhere.]
  [#*: Portable C. The same loops with the product in four 32-bit pieces and the
    carry by comparison. #~"-DZ_WORD_PORTABLE" forces it under any compiler.]

All three give the same words, and the corpus holds them to the same goldens.

tcc always takes the portable forms: it has no #~"__int128" and no carry
builtins, and compiles none of the inline asm. #~"math" is as correct under tcc
and slower; build with gcc or clang, #~"--release", for speed.

#~"make bench-math" times each operation at sizes from 1 to 100,000 words in
four builds: asm, builtins, portable and tcc, after #~"make bench-math-kernels"
has timed the word loops alone in the same four. A processor with two kinds of
core is measured on one of them, #~"BENCH_CPU" (0 by default). #~"make
test-math-arm64" builds the math corpus with #~"--target aarch64-linux-gnu" and
runs it under qemu, on the builtins and then on the asm kernels; #~"make
test-math-noasm" runs it on x86-64 with #~"-DZ_MATH_NOASM".
